  One genuinely architectural question remains: [1mhow a task change becomes a notification.[0m Everything else follows from
  it.

  [1mA. Event log at the center.[0m All task mutations funnel through one [38;5;153mapplyChange(taskId, patch, actorId)[39m that appends an
  immutable [38;5;153mTaskEvent[39m — [38;5;153m{id, taskId, actorId, type, field, from, to, at}[39m — to an append-only log. A fan-out step reads
  each event, resolves subscribers, drops the actor, and writes notification records. [3mWins when[0m you later add a backend:
  an event log is exactly the sync boundary a server needs, so the client/server split falls along a seam that already
  exists. Also gives you free per-task activity history and replayable tests — you can assert notification behavior by
  feeding events in, with no UI involved. [3mCosts:[0m two stores instead of one, and a retention policy, since the log grows
  forever.

  [1mB. Diff on write.[0m No log. The store compares previous and next task snapshots on each write, produces change
  descriptors in memory, and hands them straight to fan-out. Only notifications are persisted. [3mWins when[0m you want the
  smallest store and don't need history. [3mCosts:[0m the change detail exists only as long as the notification row it
  produced; there's no activity feed, and when the backend arrives you'll almost certainly build the event log anyway —
  this defers that work rather than avoiding it.

  [1mC. Derived on read[0m — store a per-user [38;5;153mlastSeenAt[39m and compute "what changed" by scanning tasks at render time. I'm
  [1mdiscarding[0m this one: it can't carry per-notification read state or coalescing counts, both of which you've already
  chosen.

  [1mMy recommendation: A.[0m The deciding factor isn't purity, it's that you picked "backend-shaped." The event log [3mis[0m the
  backend shape — with B, the swap-in-a-server step means inventing the event model from scratch under pressure. A's
  extra cost is one more store plus a cap (keep the last N events), which is small and self-contained.
